WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.930
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:10.740
Let's have a look at this majestic technique
that is about style transfer for photos.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:16.250
Style transfer is a magical algorithm where
we have one photograph with content, and one

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.020
with an interesting style.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:22.289
And the output is a third image with these
two photos fused together.

00:00:22.289 --> 00:00:26.470
This is typically achieved by a classical
machine learning technique that we call a

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:28.600
convolutional neural network.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:33.690
The more layers these networks contain, the
more powerful they are, and the more capable

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:37.260
they are in building an intuitive understanding
of an image.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:42.120
We had several earlier episodes on visualizing
the inner workings of these neural networks,

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.449
as always, the links are available in the
video description.

00:00:45.449 --> 00:00:50.110
Don't miss out, I am sure you'll be as amazed
by the results as I was when I have first

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:51.110
seen them.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:56.220
These previous neural style transfer techniques
work amazingly well if we're looking for a

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:57.730
painterly result.

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:03.280
However, for photo style transfer, the closeups
here reveal that they introduce unnecessary

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:04.670
distortions to the image.

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:07.210
They won't look realistic anymore.

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:09.560
But not with this new one.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:10.860
Have a look at these results.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.939
This is absolute insanity.

00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:15.920
They are just right in some sense.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.510
There is an elusive quality to them.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:19.960
And this is the challenge!

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:25.619
We not only have to put what we're searching
for into words, but we have to find a mathematical

00:01:25.619 --> 00:01:29.420
description of these words to make the computer
execute it.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.760
So what would this definition be?

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.960
Just think about this, this is a really challenging
question.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:40.960
The authors decided that the photorealism
of the output image is to be maximized.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:47.680
Well, this sounds great, but who really knows
a rigorous mathematical description of photorealism?

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.990
One possible solution would be to stipulate
that the changes in the output color would

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:57.700
have to preserve the ratios and distances
of the input style colors.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:03.270
Similar rules are used in linear algebra and
computer graphics to make sure shapes don't

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:08.700
get distorted as we're tormenting them with
rotations, translations and more.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:12.130
We like to call these operations affine transformations.

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:17.599
So the fully scientific description would
be that we add a regularization term that

00:02:17.599 --> 00:02:21.970
stipulates, that these colors only undergo
affine transformations.

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:27.260
But we've used one more new word here - what
does this regularization term mean?

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:32.370
This means that there are a ton of different
possible solutions for transferring the colors,

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:39.080
and we're trying to steer the optimizer towards
solutions that adhere to some additional criterion,

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.870
in our case, the affine transformations.

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:46.510
In the mathematical description of this problem,
these additional stipulations appear in the

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:48.769
form of a regularization term.

00:02:48.769 --> 00:02:53.980
I am so happy that you Fellow Scholars have
been watching Two Minute Papers for so long,

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.739
that we can finally talk about techniques
like this.

00:02:56.739 --> 00:03:02.370
It's fantastic to have an audience that has
this level of understanding of these topics.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.370
Love it.

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:05.060
Just absolutely love it.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.860
The source code of this project is also available.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:13.220
Also, make sure to have a look at Distill,
an absolutely amazing new science journal

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:14.390
from the Google Brain team.

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:20.000
But this is no ordinary journal, because what
they are looking for is not necessarily novel

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.750
techniques, but novel and intuitive ways of
explaining already existing works.

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:30.379
There is also an excellent write-up on research
debt that can almost be understood as a manifesto

00:03:30.379 --> 00:03:31.390
for this journal.

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:32.450
A worthy read indeed.

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:33.450
They also created a prize for science distillation.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:38.129
I love this new initiative and I am sure we'll
hear about this journal a lot in the near

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:39.129
future.

00:03:39.129 --> 00:03:42.080
Make sure to have a look, there is a link
to all of these in the video description.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:46.560
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

